<resources>
    <!-- Refresh rate controls in Settings -> Display.

         The panel exposes three modes and the framework already drives all of
         them (dumpsys display on the hardware, 2026-09-02):

             supportedModes  60.0 / 90.0 / 120.00001 Hz, all 720x1612
             mDefaultPeakRefreshRate  120.0
             mDefaultRefreshRate      0.0
             primaryRefreshRateRange  [0 120]

         so the framework side needs nothing; see the config_defaultPeakRefreshRate
         note in overlay/frameworks/base/core/res for how that was earned.

         What was missing is the UI. All three Settings gates ship FALSE upstream:

             packages/apps/Settings/res/values/lineage_config.xml:30
                 config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch   false   -> hides "max_refresh_rate"
             packages/apps/Settings/res/values/lineage_config.xml:33
                 config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch    false   -> hides "min_refresh_rate"
             packages/apps/Settings/res/values/config.xml:534
                 config_show_smooth_display             false   -> hides "peak_refresh_rate"

         and this tree carried no Settings overlay at all, so all three stayed
         false and Display had no refresh-rate entry whatsoever. Reported by a
         user 2026-09-02 ("Gadak refresh rate ya"), and the operator confirms it
         used to be present — i.e. this is a regression from the clean-room
         device-tree rewrite, which authored only the frameworks/base overlay.

         🔴 VERIFIED FROM THE SHIPPED ARTIFACT, not from the source, because a
         source default proves nothing about what got compiled in. Pulled
         /system_ext/priv-app/Settings/Settings.apk off v3build7 and read the
         values back with aapt2:

             bool/config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch    () false
             bool/config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch   () false
             bool/config_show_smooth_display             () false

         Positive control for that dump: 76 other bools in the SAME apk read
         "() true", so a false there is a real false and not a broken reader.

         config_show_smooth_display stays false deliberately: it drives a
         SwitchPreference, which is binary, and this panel has THREE rates — a
         switch cannot express 90. Turning it on would add a third control that
         silently fights the others over the same PEAK_REFRESH_RATE setting.

         🔴 UPDATED 2026-09-02 (second pass): ONE CONTROL, NOT TWO.
         config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch goes back to FALSE. v3build8 shipped
         both ListPreferences and that shipped three defects at once, all
         reported or confirmed by the operator:

           1. the peak list rendered 60, 120, 90 — unsorted. The controller adds
              Display.getSupportedModes() verbatim, and this panel enumerates in
              that order. Confirmed on hardware 2026-09-02:
                supportedModes [{id=1, fps=60.0}, {id=2, fps=120.00001},
                                {id=3, fps=90.0}]
           2. mixed language: values-in/ translates min_refresh_rate_title and
              has NO max_refresh_rate_title, so Indonesian users saw one title
              translated and one in English. Fixed by the cm_strings.xml
              override beside this file.
           3. two controls where one would do, and the two could be set to
              contradict each other (min above peak) with no UI feedback.

         The replacement is a single control whose semantics were MEASURED on
         hardware before any code was written, screen ON, reading back the
         committed mode rather than the request:

           Smart   min 0   peak 120  ->  [0 120]    committed modeId 2
           120 Hz  min 120 peak 120  ->  [120 120]  committed modeId 2
           90 Hz   min 90  peak 90   ->  [90 90]    committed modeId 3
           60 Hz   min 60  peak 60   ->  [60 60]    committed modeId 1

         The committed modeId moving is what proves these are real mode changes
         and not just a clamped range — and it settles the float question, since
         writing "120.00" correctly selects the 120.00001 mode.

         🪤 Hiding the min control is what MinRefreshRatePreferenceController's
         own gate does with this bool: with it false the controller never
         populates mEntries, and getAvailabilityStatus() returns
         UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE for mEntries.size() <= 1
         (MinRefreshRatePreferenceController.java:49,64-66). So this single line
         removes the preference; it does not merely blank it.

         ⚠ The behaviour half of this feature CANNOT live here. The entry list is
         built programmatically in the controller's constructor, so no resource
         overlay can sort it, add a "Smart" entry, or make one control write both
         MIN_ and PEAK_REFRESH_RATE. That is apply-overlays-v2 step 44, and the
         two halves must ship together: this bool alone would leave the device
         with a single UNSORTED peak-only control. -->
    <bool name="config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch">true</bool>
</resources>
